5. from Freud to Jung [elc]

The broad pattern is
that almost everyone who came after Freud
either softened his emphasis on sexuality,
expanded his focus beyond childhood, g
ave more weight to culture and society,
or — like Jung — went in a completely different mystical/symbolic direction.
Freud himself was famously bitter about most of these departures.

Carl Jung, who coined the terms introversion/extraversion,
broke from Freud and developed what he called Analytical Psychology.
His key concepts were: The Psyche's structure — instead of id/ego/superego,
Jung divided the mind into the ego (conscious self), the personal unconscious (repressed or forgotten personal experiences, similar to Freud's unconscious),
and most distinctively, t
he collective unconscious — the psychosphere
that Rust speaks about in true detective,
a deeper layer shared by all humanity,
containing inherited psychological material across generations.
David Lynch says ideas are there,
floating in this space,
maybe the psychosphere, and we just have to catch them.
I love that so much.

6. archetypes and individuation [elc]

Jung defined Archetypes
which are universal symbolic figures and patterns
living in the collective unconscious.

The main ones include the Shadow (the dark, repressed side of yourself),
the Anima/Animus (the feminine aspect in men / masculine in women),
the Persona (the mask you show the world),
and the Self (the unified whole of the psyche, the goal of psychological development).

Individuation was his version of psychological health,
meaning the lifelong process
of integrating all these parts of yourself into a coherent whole.
Roughly, becoming fully who you are.

