28. the English commons [ai]

Before enclosure,
large portions of English land were "common"
Landowners — often lords of the manor — began fencing off common land
and consolidating it into private holdings.
This happened in waves,
but the most intense period was roughly 1750–1850,
driven by Parliamentary Enclosure Acts.
Parliament passed over 5,000 individual acts
affecting millions of acres.
A commission would arrive in a village,
assess the land, and redistribute it — overwhelmingly in favor of larger landowners.

Smaller holders often received plots too small to be viable,
and the landless poor received nothing at all,
because customary rights weren't recognized as legal property.
