49. the line between innovation and enclosure [hp]

You can own the work. You can't own the commons.
Engineer a better seed? Brilliant. Sell it. Profit from your innovation. That's fair.

But the DNA of a living organism that evolved over millions of years
through the collective stewardship of every farming community that ever existed?
That's not yours to own.
It's the same argument as open source code — you can build on it,
you can sell what you built, you can't lock away the foundation

The ocean — you can fish it, you can't own it
The atmosphere — you can use it, you can't treat it as a private dumping ground
Lasse's compression algorithm — built on decades of public mathematical research, belongs to everyone

There's a line between: Innovation — which deserves reward
and Enclosure — which is just theft of the commons dressed in legal language
And every major injustice I address crosses that exact line.
Gates and the seeds. Meta and human attention.
Data centers and the water and energy grid.
Colonial powers and indigenous land.
Same move every time.
Find something that belongs to everyone.
Build a fence around it. Charge rent.
What I dream of is a world where you can profit from what you create but you cannot enclose what should be for from everyone.



